//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//
// bml-form-size-fixture.bml
//
// One statement large enough to meet the eval-depth wall if the BML
// lowering ever again holds a walker frame per character of a form.
// Ledger R91 (witnessed 2026-09-04): a `def rows() = list(...)` of 60
// rows x 500 bytes (about 30 000 normalized characters) died in the
// lowering child; 50 rows lowered. This list is 128 rows whose lengths
// run 250, 251, ... 377 bytes (row i is 250 + i bytes long, 40128 bytes
// in all) -- about 40 600 normalized characters in a single def, so the
// old wall (266 338 304 bytes of walker stack at ~8.8 KB per character)
// sits well inside it. The distinct lengths let a reader with only
// str_len prove that row 64 sits at position 64: order survived the
// lowering as well as size. The two small defs after the list pin what
// normalization keeps: space runs outside quotes collapse to one
// space; spaces and escaped quotes inside a literal stay as written.
//
// Read by tests/bml-form-size-band.fk; running this unit alone answers
// the row count: 128.
//
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

